/workflows/conversation-collaboration.md): Quote, comment on, and rate Agent replies directly in a conversation.\nL8: - [Code Review](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/code-review.md): Use the built-in Review command to inspect local code changes.\nL9: - [Permissions and Safety](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/workflows/permissions.md): Understand how MiniMax Code handles permission prompts for local files, commands, and external actions.\nL10: - [Infinite Canvas](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/infinite-canvas.md): Bring your assets, ideas, and generated results onto one canvas, and create alongside your Agent.\nL11: - [Goal](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/goal.md): Set a verifiable outcome for MiniMax Code and let it keep working until the goal is achieved or progress is blocked.\nL12: - [Built-in Browser](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/browser.md): Open pages, preview generated HTML, and debug web work inside the MiniMax Code task view.\nL13: - [Computer Use](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/computer-use.md): Let MiniMax Code operate apps on your computer directly for tasks that need a graphical interface.\nL14: - [Files / Changes / Terminal Panels](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/panels.md): Use MiniMax Code panels to inspect files, code changes, and command output.\nL15: - [Appearance](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/appearance.md): Adjust the display mode, theme, density, fonts, and colors of MiniMax Code.\nL16: - [Shortcuts and Mini Window](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/shortcuts.md): Use global shortcuts, command palette, and the mini window to work faster.\nL17: - [Updates and Feedback](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/desktop/updates-feedback.md): Check for MiniMax Code updates and submit feedback tickets from the desktop app.\nL18: - [Agent Team](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/team.md): Use Agent Team for complex tasks that benefit from decomposition, collaboration, and verification.\nL19: - [Custom Agents](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/custom-agents.md): Create dedicated Agents with their own role, instructions, skills, workspace, and channels.\nL20: - [Custom Mini Apps](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/mini-apps.md): Bring information, tools, and everyday tasks together in one interface.\nL21: - [H3 Max Video Generation](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/h3-max.md): Create videos with sound from text prompts and reference media in MiniMax Code.\nL22: - [Plugin Marketplace](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/plugins.md): Install and use domain plugins in MiniMax Code.\nL23: - [MCP Servers](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/mcp.md): Connect external tools and data sources to MiniMax Code.\nL24: - [Memory](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/agents/memory.md): Let MiniMax Code retain useful preferences, project conventions, and long-term working patterns.\nL25: - [Scheduled Tasks](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/automation/schedules.md): Let Agents run recurring work on a schedule.\nL26: - [Remote Control](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/automation/remote-control.md): Connect to your desktop Agent from your phone to check progress, add instructions, and approve permissions.\nL27: - [Messaging Integrations](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/automation/im.md): Collaborate with Agents through Telegram, WeChat, Lark, or Feishu depending on the locale.\nL28: - [M Plan and Credits](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/usage.md): View your MiniMax Code subscription plan, credit balance, and usage quotas.\nL29: - [MiniMax API Key](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/minimax-api.md): Configure your own MiniMax API key in MiniMax Code.\nL30: - [Custom Models / BYOK](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/byok.md): Add a custom model provider with base URL, API key, API format, and model names.\nL31: - [Configure BYOK Models with an Agent](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/account/byok-agent-setup.md): Configure custom models with an agent and verify real responses using curl on macOS and Windows.\nL32: - [FAQ](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/help/faq.md): Frequently asked questions about MiniMax Code desktop.\nL33: - [Feedback](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/code/help/feedback.md): Submit reproduction details, screenshots, and diagnostics when reporting issues.\nL34: - [Quick Start](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/quick-start.md): Install MiniMax Code CLI, sign in, and complete your first terminal task.\nL35: - [Features and configuration](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/features.md): Learn about MiniMax Code CLI entry points, interactive capabilities, configuration, and integrations.\nL36: - [Workflows](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/workflows.md): Use MiniMax Code CLI for repository work, multi-turn tasks, plans, permissions, and session recovery.\nL37: - [Configuration and models](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/configuration.md): Configure MiniMax Code CLI data directories, credentials, providers, models, proxies, skills, MCP, and plugins.\nL38: - [Headless and CI](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/automation.md): Use mcode exec from Shell, CI, batch jobs, and evaluations with structured output and stable exit codes.\nL39: - [ACP integration](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/integrations.md): Connect MiniMax Code CLI to editors and Agent hosts that support Agent Client Protocol.\nL40: - [Command reference](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/reference.md): Review MiniMax Code CLI top-level commands, options, and machine-readable output entry points.\nL41: - [Security and permissions](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/security.md): Understand MiniMax Code CLI permission modes, credential protection, and automation boundaries.\nL42: - [Troubleshooting](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/cli/faq.md): Troubleshoot MiniMax Code CLI installation, sign-in, configuration, automation, and terminal issues.\nL43: - [Changelog](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/changelog.md): Track every update to MiniMax Code.\nL44: - [Tech Blog](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog.md): Technical articles from the MiniMax Agent engineering team.\nL45: - [Common Issues](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/question.md): Solutions for common MiniMax Code client startup, login, unresponsive app, and log upload issues.\nL46: - [MiniMax Agent Team: Built for Long-Horizon Work, Built to Keep Improving](https://agent.minimax.io/docs/techblog/agent-team.md): Why we built Agent Team: how we designed it, what it solves, what it costs, when to use it, and when to keep it simple.\nL47: ## AsyncAPI Specs\nL48: \nL49: - [asyncapi](/docs/api-reference/speech/t2a/api/asyncapi.json)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenAI and Broadcom announce strategic collaboration to deploy 10 gigawatts of OpenAI-designed AI accelerators | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/openai-and-broadcom-announce-strategic-collaboration/)\nciteturn2686view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/openai-and-broadcom-announce-strategic-collaboration/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 138\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: October 13, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # OpenAI and Broadcom announce strategic collaboration to deploy 10 gigawatts of OpenAI-designed AI accelerators\nL17: \nL18: Multi-year partnership enables OpenAI and Broadcom to deliver accelerator and network systems for next-generation AI clusters.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: News:\nL25: \nL26:   * OpenAI and Broadcom will co-develop systems that include accelerators and Ethernet solutions from Broadcom for scale-up and scale-out\nL27:   * Broadcom to deploy racks of AI accelerator and network systems targeted to start in the second half of 2026, to complete by end of 2029\nL28: San Francisco and Palo Alto—October 13, 2025—OpenAI and Broadcom today announced a collaboration for 10 gigawatts of custom AI accelerators. OpenAI will design the accelerators and systems, which will be developed and deployed in partnership with Broadcom. By designing its own chips and systems, OpenAI can embed what it’s learned from developing frontier models and products directly into the hardware, unlocking new levels of capability and intelligence.\nL29: The racks, scaled entirely with Ethernet and other connectivity solutions from Broadcom, will meet surging global demand for AI, with deployments across OpenAI’s facilities and partner data centers.\nL30: OpenAI and Broadcom have long-standing agreements on the co-development and supply of the AI accelerators. The two companies have signed a term sheet to deploy racks incorporating the AI accelerators and Broadcom networking solutions.\nL31: “Partnering with Broadcom is a critical step in building the infrastructure needed to unlock AI’s potential and deliver real benefits for people and businesses,” said Sam Altman, co-founder and CEO of OpenAI. “Developing our own accelerators adds to the broader ecosystem of partners all building the capacity required to push the frontier of AI to provide benefits to all humanity.”\nL32: “Broadcom’s collaboration with OpenAI signifies a pivotal moment in the pursuit of artificial general intelligence,” said Hock Tan, President and CEO of Broadcom. “OpenAI has been in the forefront of the AI revolution since the ChatGPT moment, and we are thrilled to co-develop and deploy 10 gigawatts of next generation accelerators and network systems to pave the way for the future of AI.”\nL33: “Our collaboration with Broadcom will power breakthroughs in AI and bring the technology’s full potential closer to reality,” said OpenAI co-founder and President, Greg Brockman. “By building our own chip, we can embed what we’ve learned from creating frontier models and products directly into the hardware, unlocking new levels of capability and intelligence.”\nL34: “Our partnership with OpenAI continues to set new industry benchmarks for the design and deployment of open, scalable and power-efficient AI clusters,” said Charlie Kawwas, Ph. D., President of the Semiconductor Solutions Group for Broadcom. “Custom accelerators combine remarkably well with standards-based Ethernet scale-up and scale-out networking solutions to provide cost and performance optimized next generation AI infrastructure.\nL35: The racks include Broadcom’s end-to-end portfolio of Ethernet, PCIe and optical connectivity solutions, reaffirming our AI infrastructure portfolio leadership.”\nL36: For Broadcom, this collaboration reinforces the importance of custom accelerators and the choice of Ethernet as the technology for scale-up and scale-out networking in AI datacenters.\nL37: \nL38: OpenAI has grown to over 800 million weekly active users and strong adoption across global enterprises, small businesses, and developers. This collaboration will help OpenAI advance its mission to ensure that artificial general intelligence benefits all of humanity.\nL39: Listen to cite4†episode 8 of the OpenAI Podcast⁠ . Sam Altman and Greg Brockman sit down with Broadcom’s Hock Tan and Charlie Kawwas.\nL40: \nL41:   * cite5†2025 L42: ## Author\nL43: \nL44: OpenAI\nL45: ## Keep reading\nL46: \nL47: cite6†View all L48: \nL49: cite7†How Albertsons Companies is reimagining retail from the inside out CompanyOct 1, 2026 L50: \nL51: cite8†DevDay 2026 Recap CompanySep 29, 2026 L52: \nL53: cite9†Introducing dots ProductSep 29, 2026 L54: \nL55: Research\nL56: \nL57:   * cite10†Research Index L58:   * cite11†Research